.TH NTPD 8 "April 2026" "ntpd v1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ntpd \- Network Time Protocol daemon (NTPv4 server)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntpd
[\fIOPTIONS\fR]
.br
.B ntpd
\-h
.br
.B ntpd
\-v
.br
.B ntpd
\-c
\fIconfig\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ntpd
is a Network Time Protocol (NTP) version 4 server implementation in C.
It synchronizes the system clock with external NTP servers and provides
time services to NTP clients.
.P
This implementation follows RFC 5905 and related specifications for NTPv4.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " file"
Specify configuration file (default: /etc/time_sync/servers.conf)
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
Bind to specific port (default: 123)
.TP
.BI \-i " interval"
Set synchronization interval in seconds (default: 30)
.TP
.BI \-s " servers"
Specify NTP servers (comma-separated list)
.TP
.BI \-d " debug"
Enable debug logging level
.TP
.BI \-f " foreground"
Run in foreground (don't daemonize)
.TP
.BI \-h
Display help message and exit
.TP
.BI \-v
Display version information and exit
.SH CONFIGURATION FILE FORMAT
The configuration file should contain one NTP server per line:
.IP
\fIserver_name\fR:\fIport\fR
.PP
Example:
.RS
.nf
pool.ntp.org:123
time.google.com:123
pool.ntp.org:123
.RS
.RE
.PP
Supported formats:
.IP
\fIhostname\fR:\fIport\fR \- Connect to NTP server by hostname
.IP
\fIIP_address\fR:\fIport\fR \- Connect to NTP server by IP address
.IP
\fIIP_address\fR \- Connect to NTP server on default port (123)
.SH NTP PACKET FORMAT
The NTP protocol uses a 48-byte packet format (plus optional extensions):
.IP
Line 1: LI, VN, Mode (byte 0)
.IP
Line 2: Stratum (byte 1)
.IP
Line 3: Poll (bytes 2-3)
.IP
Line 4: Precision (byte 4)
.IP
Line 5: Reserved (byte 5)
.IP
Line 6: Root delay (bytes 6-9)
.IP
Line 7: Root dispersion (bytes 10-13)
.IP
Line 8: Reference ID (bytes 14-17)
.IP
Line 9: Reference timestamp (bytes 18-25)
.IP
Line 10: Originate timestamp (bytes 26-33)
.IP
Line 11: Receive timestamp (bytes 34-41)
.IP
Line 12: Transmit timestamp (bytes 42-49)
.SH TIMESTAMP FORMAT
NTP uses a 64-bit timestamp with the following structure:
.IP
Seconds since 1900-01-01T00:00:00 UTC (32 bits)
.IP
Fractional seconds (32 bits, 2^-32 precision)
.PP
The epoch is January 1, 1900, which is 2208988800 seconds before
the Unix epoch (January 1, 1970).
.SH LEAP INDICATOR
The Leap Indicator (LI) field indicates special conditions:
.IP 0
No warning
.IP 1
Last 16 minutes of month
.IP 2
Last 8 minutes of month
.IP 3
Last 4 minutes of month
.SH MODE FIELD
The Mode field indicates the type of NTP message:
.IP 1
Symmetric active
.IP 2
Symmetric passive
.IP 3
Client
.IP 4
Server
.IP 5
Broadcast
.IP 6
Control
.SH STRATUM
The Stratum field indicates the distance from the reference clock:
.IP 0
Special (reference clock)
.IP 1-15
Stratum level
.IP 16
Unsynchronized
.SH DELAY AND OFFSET CALCULATION
The following formulas are used for calculating delay and offset:
.IP
\fIdelay\fR = (T4 - T1) - (T3 - T2)
.IP
\fIoffset\fR = ½[(T2 - T1) + (T3 - T4)]
.PP
Where:
.IP
T1 = Originate Timestamp (client sends request)
.IP
T2 = Receive Timestamp (server receives request)
.IP
T3 = Transmit Timestamp (server sends response)
.IP
T4 = Destination Timestamp (client receives response)
.SH MARX FILTER
The Marx filter is used for outlier rejection:
.IP
\fithreshold\fR = median + k * MAD
.PP
Samples where \fI|delay - median| > threshold\fR are rejected.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
0
Success
.TP
1
Configuration error
.TP
2
Socket binding error
.TP
3
Server synchronization error
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
SIGHUP
Reload configuration without restarting
.TP
SIGTERM
Graceful shutdown
.TP
SIGINT
Interrupt (graceful shutdown)
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
Run as daemon with default configuration:
.RS
.nf
ntpd
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Run in foreground for debugging:
.RS
.nf
ntpd -f
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Run with custom configuration:
.RS
.nf
ntpd -c /etc/ntpd.conf
.fi
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
Run with specific port:
.RS
.nf
ntpd -p 1234
.fi
.RE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/time_sync/servers.conf
Default configuration file containing NTP server list
.TP
.B /var/log/syslog
System log containing ntpd messages
.TP
.B /var/run/ntpd.pid
Process ID file (when running as daemon)
